WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
When you're building AI applications, you'll constantly need to connect CLOD to outside

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:12.079
systems, including databases, APIs, and internal tools. Before MCP, every one of those connections

3
00:00:12.079 --> 00:00:18.159
was a custom job. In this lesson, we're going to look at what the Model Context Protocol is,

4
00:00:18.159 --> 00:00:22.319
the problem it was designed to solve, and how the client-server model works.

5
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.520
The word open is doing real work in Model Context Protocol.

6
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.720
MCP is an open standard, which means it isn't something Anthropic owns exclusively

7
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:39.119
or controls. Any AI model or platform that follows the spec can implement it. The server

8
00:00:39.119 --> 00:00:44.720
is the process that hosts capabilities and exposes them over a defined transport layer.

9
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.360
The client is whatever consumes those capabilities. In most cases, that's your

10
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.799
application or agent host, with CLOD doing the reasoning over the results it gets back.

11
00:00:54.799 --> 00:00:59.360
Protocol, server, and client are the foundation of everything else in this lesson,

12
00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:04.559
so get them locked in before you move forward, because the rest of what we cover leans on them.

13
00:01:05.839 --> 00:01:11.760
Here's the core problem MCP was built to solve. Before it existed, if you wanted CLOD to call

14
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.720
a database, you wrote custom glue code. If you wanted it to call a weather API,

15
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.239
you wrote different glue code. Every tool had its own contract, its own auth scheme,

16
00:01:22.239 --> 00:01:29.199
its own error format. When any tool updated its API, your glue code broke. You were rebuilding

17
00:01:29.199 --> 00:01:34.400
the same integration logic over and over for every new capability you added to your system.

18
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.040
MCP replaces all of that with one consistent interface that both sides agree to follow up

19
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:47.519
front before any code gets written. The before and after here is stark. Before MCP,

20
00:01:47.519 --> 00:01:53.519
you've got a growing web of custom adapters where every new tool you add increases the complexity

21
00:01:53.519 --> 00:02:00.559
and every change to a tool ripples through your code base in unpredictable ways. After MCP,

22
00:02:00.559 --> 00:02:05.760
the server exposes capabilities through a standard interface, and the client connects to it the same

23
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.520
way it connects to every other server. Swapping out the underlying tool no longer requires touching

24
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.880
your client code at all. That's where the compounding value comes from. Each new capability

25
00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:22.559
you add gets cheaper to integrate, not more expensive, because you're reusing the same contract

26
00:02:22.559 --> 00:02:29.360
every time. The architecture follows a classic client-server split, but the roles are worth being

27
00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:36.399
precise about. The server is the provider. It hosts capabilities and waits for requests. The client

28
00:02:36.399 --> 00:02:41.839
connects to one or more servers, negotiates capabilities during the initialize handshake,

29
00:02:41.839 --> 00:02:47.600
then calls each server's list methods to see what's available and routes requests to the right

30
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:54.240
server at the right time. One client can connect to multiple servers simultaneously, so your

31
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.360
application might have a database server, a web search server, and an internal knowledge-based

32
00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:04.399
server all running at the same time, and your client talks to all of them through the same

33
00:03:04.399 --> 00:03:09.919
protocol. That separation keeps capability providers completely independent of the consumers.

34
00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:16.240
One of the strongest design decisions in MCP is that it's fully model-agnostic. The protocol

35
00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:22.160
doesn't care whether clawed, another LLM or a local open-source model is on the client side.

36
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.639
Servers don't know or care which model is consuming them. Clients don't know which vendor built the

37
00:03:28.639 --> 00:03:34.639
server. That means you can build an MCP server for your internal tools today, and it'll work with

38
00:03:35.119 --> 00:03:40.800
whatever AI model your team adopts next year, without any rework. You're not locked into

39
00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:47.600
Anthropic or any other vendor. That vendor-neutral composability is what makes MCP worth investing in

40
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.679
as foundational infrastructure for your entire AI application stack.

41
00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:59.119
When Clawed connects to an MCP server, it's acting as the MCP client. It opens a connection

42
00:03:59.119 --> 00:04:03.839
over the configured transport layer, which is just the channel the bytes move across,

43
00:04:03.839 --> 00:04:10.720
usually standard IO for local servers or streamable HTTP for remote ones. Streamable

44
00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.880
HTTP can optionally use server sent events to stream messages back, and the older standalone

45
00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:23.760
HTTP plus SSE transport is now deprecated. Client and server first negotiate capabilities

46
00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.839
during the initialized handshake, declaring which features each supports. Then Clawed runs

47
00:04:29.839 --> 00:04:36.480
capability discovery, calling the server's tools list, resources list, and prompts list methods

48
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:42.320
to learn what's available. All of it loads into Clawed's context, where Clawed reasons over it

49
00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:46.880
like any other information, and decides what the conversation actually needs.

50
00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:54.959
In production, MCP lets teams work independently without tight coupling between them. A database

51
00:04:54.959 --> 00:05:01.119
team ships an MCP server for their data layer. A search team ships a completely separate server.

52
00:05:01.119 --> 00:05:06.079
The application team doesn't need to understand the internals of either one. They just connect

53
00:05:06.079 --> 00:05:10.959
and route calls through the protocol. When the database team changes their implementation or

54
00:05:10.959 --> 00:05:16.399
migrates to a new system, they update their server. The client contract stays stable and

55
00:05:16.399 --> 00:05:22.640
nothing downstream breaks. This contract first approach is how you scale a complex AI system

56
00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:27.440
across a large organization without the whole thing becoming tightly coupled code

57
00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:35.679
that breaks every time any dependency changes. MCP gives every tool the same front door, so your

58
00:05:35.679 --> 00:05:40.799
application talks to a database server the same way it talks to a search server. That matters

59
00:05:40.799 --> 00:05:47.119
because integration cost stops growing with every capability you add. Keep in mind, the protocol

60
00:05:47.119 --> 00:05:52.880
standardizes the conversation, not the quality of what's on the other end. A poorly designed

61
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:58.640
server stays poorly designed. And that closes out this lesson. I'll see you in the next video.

